+We have two practical outcomes from the analysis. Since $N_{pop}$ is the only hyperparameter with a meaningful, scale-dependent effect, it is also the only one that we tune per instance: the main experiments do not adopt a single fixed population size, but select $N_{pop}$ for every instance and every algorithm through the adaptive bracket-and-bisect line search described in Section~\ref{sec:experiments}, starting from $N_{pop,0} = 75$ for GA/EGA and $N_{pop,0} = 50$ for PSO. The selected values, reported in Table~\ref{tab:popsize_selected}, are systematically larger than the $N_{pop} = 30$ default used in this one-factor-at-a-time analysis. All remaining hyperparameters are kept at their default values. The absence of a systematic trend in $G_{max}$, $N_{eval}$, $c_{rate}$, and $m_{rate}$ suggests that a full grid or Bayesian search over these parameters would not have yielded materially different conclusions, only longer training times. Any future, more resource-intensive hyperparameter tuning for these methods should therefore focus on $N_{pop}$, and possibly on its interaction with instance scale, rather than on the remaining four hyperparameters. This also justifies the decision not to perform an exhaustive grid search per instance, which would be incompatible with the computational budget reported in Section~\ref{sec:experiments}.}
 
